MENTOR: So Lid turns from its center.

Right now Lid's pivot is outside the lid, below it, which is why it would swing wide if you turned it now. Click Lid in the timeline and its crosshair shows up below the shape; start your drag there.

Here's the idea behind all of this: **Anchor Point is where the pivot sits inside the layer; Position is where that pivot sits in the comp.** If you change Anchor Point by itself, the layer slides so the new pivot lands where the old one was. Pan Behind changes both together, so nothing moves on screen.

At 0 s, Lid is a short, wide bar in the upper left of the frame. Once its pivot is in the middle of that bar, it will spin in place instead of swinging around a point below it.

Here's how to set it:
1. Press **Y** to get the Pan Behind tool.
2. Click Lid in the timeline so its crosshair shows up, below the shape.
3. Start your drag right on that crosshair and drag it to the middle of Lid.
4. To watch both numbers change while you drag, select Lid and press **A**, then **Shift+P**.

There's also a menu command just for the center: Layer > Transform > Center Anchor Point in Layer Content. It puts the pivot in the middle without Lid moving. Either way works.

Set Lid's pivot yourself, then tell me "done".

I could show you on Door first, if you like.

